  "worked_solution": "Since $u=5x-3$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{u+3}{5}.$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $-3$ | $7$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{7}\\frac{u+3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}(u+3){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}({u}^{3/2}+3{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$",
